// A lyrics row under a LONE tab staff, with every fret inside the strings. The
// distance from the tab's reference point to the syllable baseline is measured
// against its own line span — a six-string tab's outermost string is 3.750000
// below that point, not the 2.000000 a five-line staff would be — so the row has
// to clear the BOTTOM STRING and not the line where a notation staff would end.
// Seeded from a user report (2026-08-29): the syllables of a system whose frets
// all sat inside the strings were engraved ON the strings, while the same book's
// other systems, which had a fret hanging below the staff, looked right — that
// ink was hiding a floor that was 1.750000 too high.
// MEASURED on LilyPond 2.26.0 (probe tab-lyric-row.ly, book TBL1): reference point
// to syllable baseline 6.120115 = the staff symbol's own ink 3.800000 + the
// syllable's ascender 1.820098 + padding 0.500000. Lily# reads 6.120000, and read
// 4.370000 before — a nominal five-line staff's 2.050000 in the same expression.

title "Tab with lyrics inside the strings"
key g major

part m { clef treble }

section A { m { g'4 g f f | } }

lyrics v { section A { Twin- kle twin- kle | } }

form main { A }

score main {
  tab m
  lyrics v sings m
}
